    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-253044",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Consider the set $A=(-\\infty ,-1).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded below, $A$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=-1.$",
